{"title":"2024 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 25","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2024 AMC 10A #25</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2024 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 22\">2024 AMC 12A #22</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_25\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 25</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Best_Motivated)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Best Motivated)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Cheese)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Cheese)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Observation)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Observation)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_By_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution By SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_solution_by_TheNeuralMathAcademy\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video solution by TheNeuralMathAcademy</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_solution_by_MaffyTaffy\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video solution by MaffyTaffy</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_25\">Problem 25</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 25\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The figure below shows a dotted grid <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cells wide and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cells tall consisting of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/e/f0eef328050ddc16d1b23dcb74f639d6cc51929d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1&#39;&#39;\\times1&#39;&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"59\" height=\"17\" /> squares. Carl places <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-inch toothpicks along some of the sides of the squares to create a closed loop that does not intersect itself. The numbers in the cells indicate the number of sides of that square that are to be covered by toothpicks, and any number of toothpicks are allowed if no number is written. In how many ways can Carl place the toothpicks?\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/1/9d176dfd41e3e8e875c6b5d84fdb4f5e4f7d43a6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(6cm); for (int i=0; i&lt;9; ++i) {   draw((i,0)--(i,3),dotted); } for (int i=0; i&lt;4; ++i){   draw((0,i)--(8,i),dotted); } for (int i=0; i&lt;8; ++i) {   for (int j=0; j&lt;3; ++j) {     if (j==1) {       label(&quot;1&quot;,(i+0.5,1.5)); }}} [/asy]\" width=\"285\" height=\"108\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee7520b35052b8077ba79fd898b9bef30ce7e82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }130\\qquad\\textbf{(B) }144\\qquad\\textbf{(C) }146\\qquad\\textbf{(D) }162\\qquad\\textbf{(E) }196$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"446\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Best_Motivated.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Best_Motivated)\">Solution 1 (Best Motivated)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Best Motivated)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Observations: \n</p><p>1. You can’t have a vertical line in any place other than the first two columns and the last two columns. If we did, we would then have at least one of the middle cells with toothpicks along more than one side, which would violate the conditions of the problem.\n</p><p>2. There are two cases that look completely different. We can have a long horizontal box that spans all eight cells either on top of or below the middle cells, or we have to have a shape that looks like a rectangle, except with a few places \"pushed\" in. \n</p><p>Thus, using casework, we can split the task of finding those rectangles with squiggly edges into 3 cases.\n</p><p>For case 1, we just assume that the green lines shown below are given (always have toothpicks on them). In effect, we will use all eight columns.\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Scrasdfasd.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/5/52/Scrasdfasd.png/600px-Scrasdfasd.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"263\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/5/52/Scrasdfasd.png 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Screenshot_2024-11-08_192200.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/0/05/Screenshot_2024-11-08_192200.png/600px-Screenshot_2024-11-08_192200.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"235\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/0/05/Screenshot_2024-11-08_192200.png 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>The only toothpicks we can place that will connect to the red lines are to go horizontally inward:\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Screenshot_2024-11-08_192516.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/2e/Screenshot_2024-11-08_192516.png/600px-Screenshot_2024-11-08_192516.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"283\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/2/2e/Screenshot_2024-11-08_192516.png 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Now, concentrate on the first row of squares. A toothpick can be placed on either the bottom or top and connected to a continuous squiggle by adding vertical toothpicks:\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Screenshot_2024-11-08_193401.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/6/66/Screenshot_2024-11-08_193401.png/600px-Screenshot_2024-11-08_193401.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"242\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/6/66/Screenshot_2024-11-08_193401.png/900px-Screenshot_2024-11-08_193401.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/6/66/Screenshot_2024-11-08_193401.png 2x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>How many squiggles are possible?\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Screenshot_2024-11-08_194804.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/2d/Screenshot_2024-11-08_194804.png/600px-Screenshot_2024-11-08_194804.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"313\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/2/2d/Screenshot_2024-11-08_194804.png 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>We can summarize this by giving a high squiggle position a 1 and a low position a 0, thus we have a 6-digit binary sequence. Thus, we can have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/3/c23f7dacd533c95a26bd926214b42d6cc00fb29f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^6=64$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"59\" height=\"15\" /> ways to make this squiggle. (The binary is not absolutely necessary, but it works.)  \n</p><p>Case 2: We can also pull in one of the sides, thus we can have a squiggle with 5 binary digits, which only uses the first or last 7 columns:\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Screenshot_2024-11-08_195135.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f1/Screenshot_2024-11-08_195135.png/600px-Screenshot_2024-11-08_195135.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"306\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f1/Screenshot_2024-11-08_195135.png/900px-Screenshot_2024-11-08_195135.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/f/f1/Screenshot_2024-11-08_195135.png 2x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Screenshot_2024-11-08_195553.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/3/3b/Screenshot_2024-11-08_195553.png/600px-Screenshot_2024-11-08_195553.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"311\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/3/3b/Screenshot_2024-11-08_195553.png 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Here, we only have 5 binary digits to work with, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/4/3a41b738c2ff69ccfd120a11701fa802704e2439.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^5=32$\" width=\"58\" height=\"15\" /> ways to make this squiggle for each individual subcase. There are two subcases, one with the first 7 columns, and the other with the last 7, so we have a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/7/a67bab00568e1d171f23ec25efd6ae291e65d976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$32\\cdot 2 = 64$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"85\" height=\"14\" /> arrangements in this case.\n</p><p>Case 3: We can use an even smaller section. Using only the middle 6 columns gives us a 4-wide squiggle:\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Screenshot_2024-11-08_195945.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/25/Screenshot_2024-11-08_195945.png/600px-Screenshot_2024-11-08_195945.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"322\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/2/25/Screenshot_2024-11-08_195945.png/900px-Screenshot_2024-11-08_195945.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/2/25/Screenshot_2024-11-08_195945.png 2x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Thus, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/8/9584bdb500d49089d884caad5e9f4484b6267bbf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^4=16$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"58\" height=\"15\" /> ways to make this squiggle. \n</p><p>These three cases together cover all loops of this form. If we try to bring the square bracket like shapes on each side any closer, there will be some middle cells that do not touch any toothpicks. Adding up all our cases for these types of shapes: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/0/9c0a090e432449fcd71d9ef7828baa45f3795ba3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$64+32+32+16=144$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"193\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>However, there are two more ways to draw a qualifying shape:\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Screenshot_2024-11-08_200416.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/e/ea/Screenshot_2024-11-08_200416.png/600px-Screenshot_2024-11-08_200416.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"236\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/e/ea/Screenshot_2024-11-08_200416.png 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>We can draw a rectangle like that in the first row or third row. Thus, we have a total of \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/b/81b4f6ed39a49ef82c06d4f4bc1a39d1bc4f01b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$144+2=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }146}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"158\" height=\"30\" /> ways.\n</p><p>~RayrayX \n</p><p><br />\nA note to (potential) editors:\nThis answer was not made to be concise or especially professional. It was made to explicitly explain this problem in a way so that it is easy to understand and follow.\n</p><p>~hermanboxcar5\n</p><p>Notes:\nRemember these are the ONLY possible cases. It is impossible to cross through the rows of boxes of ones (in a snake like pattern) to connect the loop around the bottom since then the loop would intersect itself. \n</p><p>We are not undercounting by only counting the binary digits on the top. Consider the top squiggle. Each middle square must and can only come in contact with one toothpick, so if the top squiggle doesn't touch a specific middle cell, the bottom squiggle must to ensure that all squares touch one toothpick.\n~juwushu\n</p><p>~LeonQS (minor edits)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Cheese.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Cheese)\">Solution 2 (Cheese)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Cheese)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that for any case where the closed loop does not connect from the top side of the ones and bottom side of the ones, there are two of these cases. A cheese solution can be found from this; noting that B and C are the only two options close to each other, and, being two apart, with people likely to forget this case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/4/634866c7d05892bcdb5eebe797613811786b1061.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }146}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"75\" height=\"30\" /> is likely to be the correct answer.\nCheese solution done by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Juwushu\" title=\"User:Juwushu\">juwushu</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Observation.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Observation)\">Solution 3 (Observation)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Observation)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> cases where the loop does not go through the middle. \n</p><p>If the loop goes through the middle, we must have a full column on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/d/41d1dd540e69bdaa243d88eb1ac40237d7da8f8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,8),(0,7),(1,8),(1,7).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"193\" height=\"20\" /> Then we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/5/565ce55f7bf6df6fed3612cb142ca0d26520bd50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6,5,5,4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"17\" /> empty middle squares. For each one we can have one on top or one on bottom, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/3/5e348eac9b255de0535cc6913996dd1e7eca2b01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^6+2^5+2^5+2^4=144.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"191\" height=\"18\" /> Notice that for each case of fixed toothpicks, there is only one way to form the loop. Then we just add <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/2/862782a3d49176eb06ad1b1923ba688eb2286edd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2+144=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } 146.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"163\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~nevergonnagiveup\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Power Solve\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FRNbJ5wIGRo\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FRNbJ5wIGRo</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_By_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution By SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution By SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"h